1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,059
The same chocolate can set six different ways,

2
00:00:03,059 --> 00:00:06,118
and only one of them snaps. Here is

3
00:00:06,118 --> 00:00:09,177
the whole video in three sentences. Chocolate snaps

4
00:00:09,177 --> 00:00:12,236
because its fat is set in one chosen

5
00:00:12,236 --> 00:00:13,765
crystal, called form five.

6
00:00:13,925 --> 00:00:16,482
Bread rises because living yeast breathes carbon

7
00:00:16,482 --> 00:00:19,040
dioxide into a web of gluten. And

8
00:00:19,040 --> 00:00:21,597
steak browns because sugars and amino acids

9
00:00:21,597 --> 00:00:23,789
react on its hot, dry surface.

10
00:00:24,339 --> 00:00:27,555
None of this was found by tasting. It

11
00:00:27,555 --> 00:00:30,770
was found with instruments that never tasted anything.

12
00:00:30,770 --> 00:00:33,986
A beam of X-rays in 1966, which told

13
00:00:33,986 --> 00:00:36,398
six crystals of cocoa butter apart.

14
00:00:36,558 --> 00:00:39,519
A microscope in 1857, when Louis Pasteur

15
00:00:39,519 --> 00:00:42,479
argued that fermentation is the work of

16
00:00:42,479 --> 00:00:45,440
living cells. And a flask in 1912,

17
00:00:45,440 --> 00:00:48,400
when Louis-Camille Maillard heated sugars with amino

18
00:00:48,400 --> 00:00:50,515
acids and watched them brown.

19
00:00:51,065 --> 00:00:53,363
Taste was where every one of these

20
00:00:53,363 --> 00:00:55,662
questions started. A chocolate maker knew a

21
00:00:55,662 --> 00:00:57,960
good snap, a baker knew a good

22
00:00:57,960 --> 00:01:00,258
rise, and a cook knew a good

23
00:01:00,258 --> 00:01:02,228
crust, for centuries, without knowing why.

24
00:01:02,388 --> 00:01:04,688
The why had to wait for a way

25
00:01:04,688 --> 00:01:06,987
to look inside: at the way fat stacks

26
00:01:06,987 --> 00:01:09,287
itself into a crystal, at a single living

27
00:01:09,287 --> 00:01:10,724
cell, at a single molecule.

28
00:01:11,274 --> 00:01:13,621
By the end, you will know why a

29
00:01:13,621 --> 00:01:15,968
melted bar never snaps again, why every hole

30
00:01:15,968 --> 00:01:18,316
in your bread was breathed out by something

31
00:01:18,316 --> 00:01:20,663
alive, and why the best part of a

32
00:01:20,663 --> 00:01:22,130
steak never reaches its middle.

33
00:01:22,290 --> 00:01:26,066
Three questions, three instruments. Chocolate first.

34
00:01:26,616 --> 00:01:29,789
Bread, steak, chocolate. What's inside?

35
00:01:30,333 --> 00:01:32,277
Why does chocolate melt in your

36
00:01:32,277 --> 00:01:34,221
mouth but not in your hand?

37
00:01:34,771 --> 00:01:37,263
The same chocolate can set six different ways,

38
00:01:37,263 --> 00:01:39,755
and only one of them snaps. That one

39
00:01:39,755 --> 00:01:42,247
is called form five. It is the crystal

40
00:01:42,247 --> 00:01:44,739
your chocolate is supposed to be made of.

41
00:01:44,899 --> 00:01:47,586
Form five melts at about thirty-four degrees

42
00:01:47,586 --> 00:01:50,273
Celsius, just below the temperature inside your

43
00:01:50,273 --> 00:01:52,576
mouth. That is the whole trick.

44
00:01:52,736 --> 00:01:55,243
Cocoa butter, the fat in chocolate, sits solid

45
00:01:55,243 --> 00:01:57,751
in a cool room, holds on in your

46
00:01:57,751 --> 00:02:00,258
hand, and gives way on your tongue, because

47
00:02:00,258 --> 00:02:02,139
one crystal was chosen for it.

48
00:02:02,299 --> 00:02:05,049
The question is how anyone could see a

49
00:02:05,049 --> 00:02:07,456
crystal in a chocolate bar at all.

50
00:02:08,006 --> 00:02:09,962
Look at the bar on the screen.

51
00:02:09,962 --> 00:02:11,918
Most of what holds it together is

52
00:02:11,918 --> 00:02:13,874
not sugar. It is cocoa butter, the

53
00:02:13,874 --> 00:02:15,830
pale fat pressed out of cacao beans.

54
00:02:15,990 --> 00:02:18,773
Sugar and cocoa solids are suspended in that

55
00:02:18,773 --> 00:02:21,556
fat, the way sand is held in wax.

56
00:02:21,556 --> 00:02:24,338
So when chocolate melts, it is really the

57
00:02:24,338 --> 00:02:27,121
fat that melts. Everything else simply lets go.

58
00:02:27,281 --> 00:02:29,406
Hold on to that idea, because it

59
00:02:29,406 --> 00:02:31,531
is the key to the whole question.

60
00:02:32,081 --> 00:02:35,356
Cocoa butter is made mostly of molecules called

61
00:02:35,356 --> 00:02:38,631
triglycerides. Each one is a small backbone, glycerol,

62
00:02:38,631 --> 00:02:41,906
carrying three long chains, the fatty acids. In

63
00:02:41,906 --> 00:02:45,182
cocoa butter the two outer chains are usually

64
00:02:45,182 --> 00:02:47,638
saturated, palmitic acid or stearic acid.

65
00:02:47,798 --> 00:02:50,857
The middle chain is usually oleic acid. That

66
00:02:50,857 --> 00:02:53,916
pattern is unusually tidy. Most fats are a

67
00:02:53,916 --> 00:02:56,974
messy crowd of different shapes. Cocoa butter is

68
00:02:56,974 --> 00:02:58,886
a crowd of near-identical ones.

69
00:02:59,436 --> 00:03:02,073
Tidy molecules can stack. When warm cocoa

70
00:03:02,073 --> 00:03:04,710
butter cools, those triglycerides line up and

71
00:03:04,710 --> 00:03:07,347
lock together into crystals, layer on layer,

72
00:03:07,347 --> 00:03:09,607
like folded chairs in a stack.

73
00:03:09,767 --> 00:03:12,020
And because they are so alike, they

74
00:03:12,020 --> 00:03:14,273
tend to let go together, over a

75
00:03:14,273 --> 00:03:16,526
narrow band of temperature, rather than slowly

76
00:03:16,526 --> 00:03:18,135
softening the way butter does.

77
00:03:18,685 --> 00:03:21,605
Here is the surprise. Those molecules can stack

78
00:03:21,605 --> 00:03:24,525
in more than one way. Packed loosely, they

79
00:03:24,525 --> 00:03:27,445
make a crystal that melts easily. Packed tightly,

80
00:03:27,445 --> 00:03:30,365
they make a crystal that needs more heat.

81
00:03:30,365 --> 00:03:32,920
Cocoa butter has six of these arrangements.

82
00:03:33,080 --> 00:03:36,061
Chemists number them from one to six, loosest

83
00:03:36,061 --> 00:03:39,043
to tightest. Picture the bar cut open, with

84
00:03:39,043 --> 00:03:42,024
the form five crystals glowing in the break.

85
00:03:42,574 --> 00:03:45,789
In 1966, two chemists, Wille and Lutton, published

86
00:03:45,789 --> 00:03:49,005
a careful study of cocoa butter in the

87
00:03:49,005 --> 00:03:52,220
journal of the American Oil Chemists' Society. They

88
00:03:52,220 --> 00:03:55,436
cooled and warmed samples under controlled conditions and

89
00:03:55,436 --> 00:03:58,249
sorted the solid fat into six forms.

90
00:03:58,409 --> 00:04:01,329
They reported a melting point for each. Form

91
00:04:01,329 --> 00:04:04,250
one melted at about seventeen degrees Celsius. Form

92
00:04:04,250 --> 00:04:07,170
five at about thirty-three point eight. Form six

93
00:04:07,170 --> 00:04:08,995
at about thirty-six point three.

94
00:04:09,545 --> 00:04:12,386
So form five melts at about thirty-four

95
00:04:12,386 --> 00:04:15,226
degrees Celsius. It sits just under the

96
00:04:15,226 --> 00:04:18,067
warmth of a living body. Solid on

97
00:04:18,067 --> 00:04:20,907
the shelf. Solid, for a while, in

98
00:04:20,907 --> 00:04:23,342
your fingers. Liquid in your mouth.

99
00:04:23,892 --> 00:04:26,949
Nobody can see these crystals with their eyes.

100
00:04:26,949 --> 00:04:30,005
They are far too small. Wille and Lutton

101
00:04:30,005 --> 00:04:33,062
used X-ray diffraction. You shine a narrow beam

102
00:04:33,062 --> 00:04:34,972
of X-rays through the fat.

103
00:04:35,132 --> 00:04:37,337
The regularly spaced layers of molecules scatter

104
00:04:37,337 --> 00:04:39,542
the beam, and the scattered rays land

105
00:04:39,542 --> 00:04:41,746
on a detector as a pattern of

106
00:04:41,746 --> 00:04:43,951
rings. The spacing of the rings depends

107
00:04:43,951 --> 00:04:46,156
on how far apart the layers are.

108
00:04:46,706 --> 00:04:48,894
A loose crystal leaves one pattern. A

109
00:04:48,894 --> 00:04:51,082
tight one leaves another. So each of

110
00:04:51,082 --> 00:04:53,269
the six forms has its own fingerprint.

111
00:04:53,269 --> 00:04:55,457
You do not need to see a

112
00:04:55,457 --> 00:04:57,645
crystal to know which one you have.

113
00:04:57,805 --> 00:05:00,189
You only need to read its rings.

114
00:05:00,739 --> 00:05:02,993
On the screen, the chocolate keeps pouring.

115
00:05:02,993 --> 00:05:05,247
Somewhere inside a bar like this, layer

116
00:05:05,247 --> 00:05:07,500
on layer of molecules sit stacked in

117
00:05:07,500 --> 00:05:09,754
rows, far too fine to see, and

118
00:05:09,754 --> 00:05:12,008
a beam of X-rays can read them.

119
00:05:12,558 --> 00:05:15,291
The second instrument measures melting directly. It

120
00:05:15,291 --> 00:05:18,024
is called differential scanning calorimetry. A few

121
00:05:18,024 --> 00:05:20,757
milligrams of fat sit in a tiny

122
00:05:20,757 --> 00:05:23,490
pan, beside an empty pan. Both are

123
00:05:23,490 --> 00:05:25,833
warmed at the same steady rate.

124
00:05:25,993 --> 00:05:28,566
The machine records how much extra heat

125
00:05:28,566 --> 00:05:31,139
the sample pan needs to keep pace.

126
00:05:31,689 --> 00:05:34,244
While the fat is simply warming, the

127
00:05:34,244 --> 00:05:36,798
extra heat is small and steady. Then

128
00:05:36,798 --> 00:05:39,353
a crystal form reaches its melting point.

129
00:05:39,353 --> 00:05:41,907
Now the heat goes into breaking the

130
00:05:41,907 --> 00:05:44,462
crystal apart, not into raising the temperature.

131
00:05:44,622 --> 00:05:47,174
The sample pan falls behind, the machine pushes

132
00:05:47,174 --> 00:05:49,727
in more heat, and the trace rises into

133
00:05:49,727 --> 00:05:52,279
a peak. The peak sits at the melting

134
00:05:52,279 --> 00:05:54,832
point of that form. Its size tells you

135
00:05:54,832 --> 00:05:57,065
how much of that form was there.

136
00:05:57,615 --> 00:05:59,929
That is why good chocolate can feel faintly

137
00:05:59,929 --> 00:06:02,243
cool on the tongue. As form five melts

138
00:06:02,243 --> 00:06:04,557
in your mouth, it is drawing heat from

139
00:06:04,557 --> 00:06:06,871
you to do it, just as the sample

140
00:06:06,871 --> 00:06:08,607
pan drew heat from the machine.

141
00:06:09,157 --> 00:06:12,057
So why does the hand lose? Partly temperature.

142
00:06:12,057 --> 00:06:14,957
The skin of your palm usually runs cooler

143
00:06:14,957 --> 00:06:17,858
than the inside of your mouth. Partly contact.

144
00:06:17,858 --> 00:06:20,758
Your fingers touch only the outside of the

145
00:06:20,758 --> 00:06:22,208
bar, through dry skin.

146
00:06:22,368 --> 00:06:25,015
Your tongue wraps the piece in warm saliva

147
00:06:25,015 --> 00:06:27,662
and presses it against the roof of your

148
00:06:27,662 --> 00:06:29,978
mouth. Heat flows in from every side.

149
00:06:30,528 --> 00:06:32,713
Hold a square long enough on a

150
00:06:32,713 --> 00:06:34,898
warm day and it will give way

151
00:06:34,898 --> 00:06:37,083
in your hand too. Form five is

152
00:06:37,083 --> 00:06:38,331
not refusing to melt.

153
00:06:38,491 --> 00:06:40,619
It is waiting for enough warmth, delivered

154
00:06:40,619 --> 00:06:42,747
fast enough, and a mouth delivers it

155
00:06:42,747 --> 00:06:44,267
far better than a palm.

156
00:06:44,817 --> 00:06:47,216
Watch the chocolate on the screen running in

157
00:06:47,216 --> 00:06:49,616
slow folds. Above its melting point it has

158
00:06:49,616 --> 00:06:52,015
no crystal at all. It is only fat,

159
00:06:52,015 --> 00:06:54,414
sugar and cocoa, flowing, and waiting for something

160
00:06:54,414 --> 00:06:56,214
to tell it how to set.

161
00:06:56,764 --> 00:06:59,349
Left to itself, cooling chocolate does not

162
00:06:59,349 --> 00:07:01,933
reliably choose form five. It sets as

163
00:07:01,933 --> 00:07:04,518
a mixture of looser forms, and the

164
00:07:04,518 --> 00:07:07,102
result is soft, dull and greasy to

165
00:07:07,102 --> 00:07:09,687
the touch. So chocolate makers temper it.

166
00:07:09,847 --> 00:07:12,237
They melt it completely, erasing every crystal.

167
00:07:12,237 --> 00:07:14,628
They cool it until crystals begin to

168
00:07:14,628 --> 00:07:17,018
form. Then they warm it slightly, just

169
00:07:17,018 --> 00:07:19,409
enough to melt the looser forms away,

170
00:07:19,409 --> 00:07:21,799
and leave form five behind as seeds.

171
00:07:22,349 --> 00:07:25,103
Those seeds teach the rest of the chocolate

172
00:07:25,103 --> 00:07:27,857
how to set. Every new crystal grows on

173
00:07:27,857 --> 00:07:30,611
the pattern of the old ones. Watch the

174
00:07:30,611 --> 00:07:33,365
chocolate on the screen slowly fold and settle.

175
00:07:33,525 --> 00:07:35,395
When it cools, the whole bar is

176
00:07:35,395 --> 00:07:36,997
form five, all the way through.

177
00:07:37,547 --> 00:07:39,839
You may already have run this experiment by

178
00:07:39,839 --> 00:07:42,131
accident. A bar left in a warm car

179
00:07:42,131 --> 00:07:44,423
melts, and when it cools it sets again.

180
00:07:44,423 --> 00:07:46,715
But it does not come back the same.

181
00:07:46,875 --> 00:07:49,831
It is softer, duller, sometimes crumbly, and it

182
00:07:49,831 --> 00:07:52,787
smears on your fingers. Nothing was added and

183
00:07:52,787 --> 00:07:55,743
nothing was taken away. The fat simply set

184
00:07:55,743 --> 00:07:58,699
without a guide, and chose the looser forms.

185
00:07:59,249 --> 00:08:02,149
That is the strongest everyday evidence that the

186
00:08:02,149 --> 00:08:05,049
crystal, not the recipe, decides how chocolate behaves.

187
00:08:05,049 --> 00:08:07,949
The same molecules, in the same proportions, packed

188
00:08:07,949 --> 00:08:10,486
a different way, make a different solid.

189
00:08:10,646 --> 00:08:12,409
To get the snap back, the whole

190
00:08:12,409 --> 00:08:14,171
bar would have to be melted again

191
00:08:14,171 --> 00:08:15,430
and tempered from the start.

192
00:08:15,980 --> 00:08:18,550
Look at the melted chocolate on the screen.

193
00:08:18,550 --> 00:08:21,120
Right now it remembers nothing. Whatever crystal it

194
00:08:21,120 --> 00:08:23,690
held before is gone, and the next one

195
00:08:23,690 --> 00:08:25,617
depends entirely on how it cools.

196
00:08:26,167 --> 00:08:29,010
You can hear form five. Its crystals are

197
00:08:29,010 --> 00:08:31,853
small, dense and evenly packed, so a tempered

198
00:08:31,853 --> 00:08:34,696
bar breaks with a clean snap instead of

199
00:08:34,696 --> 00:08:36,828
bending. You can see it too.

200
00:08:36,988 --> 00:08:39,336
A surface made of small, even crystals reflects

201
00:08:39,336 --> 00:08:41,684
light like a mirror, which is the gloss.

202
00:08:41,684 --> 00:08:44,032
And as the bar sets, it shrinks slightly,

203
00:08:44,032 --> 00:08:46,380
which is how it releases from its mould.

204
00:08:46,930 --> 00:08:49,281
Look at the wall of pieces on the

205
00:08:49,281 --> 00:08:51,633
screen. Every one of them was melted, cooled

206
00:08:51,633 --> 00:08:53,984
with care and set in the same crystal.

207
00:08:53,984 --> 00:08:56,335
Break any one of them and you would

208
00:08:56,335 --> 00:08:57,805
hear the same clean snap.

209
00:08:58,355 --> 00:09:00,805
The glowing line in the cross-section is a

210
00:09:00,805 --> 00:09:03,254
drawing, not a photograph, laid over a computer-made

211
00:09:03,254 --> 00:09:05,704
picture. The crystals are real, and they are

212
00:09:05,704 --> 00:09:07,235
too small for any camera.

213
00:09:07,395 --> 00:09:09,566
Every pour, drip and bar you

214
00:09:09,566 --> 00:09:11,736
see is real footage, repeated into

215
00:09:11,736 --> 00:09:13,907
walls and mirrors to show scale.

216
00:09:14,457 --> 00:09:17,597
The melting points belong to pure cocoa butter,

217
00:09:17,597 --> 00:09:20,737
measured in a laboratory. A real bar also

218
00:09:20,737 --> 00:09:23,877
holds sugar, cocoa solids and, often, milk fat,

219
00:09:23,877 --> 00:09:26,625
and those shift the numbers a little.

220
00:09:26,785 --> 00:09:29,603
Researchers have also argued about how best to

221
00:09:29,603 --> 00:09:32,420
divide and name the forms. Six is the

222
00:09:32,420 --> 00:09:34,886
standard convention, not a law of nature.

223
00:09:35,436 --> 00:09:38,341
And form five does not last forever. Over

224
00:09:38,341 --> 00:09:41,246
months, or after a warm day and a

225
00:09:41,246 --> 00:09:44,151
cool night, it can slowly rearrange into form

226
00:09:44,151 --> 00:09:47,055
six. The surface turns dull and streaked with

227
00:09:47,055 --> 00:09:48,871
grey. That is fat bloom.

228
00:09:49,031 --> 00:09:51,332
The chocolate is still chocolate. Its

229
00:09:51,332 --> 00:09:53,249
crystal has simply moved on.

230
00:09:53,799 --> 00:09:56,229
So the next time a square of chocolate

231
00:09:56,229 --> 00:09:58,658
disappears on your tongue and refuses to do

232
00:09:58,658 --> 00:10:01,088
the same in your hand, you can thank

233
00:10:01,088 --> 00:10:03,518
a crystal you will never see, two chemists

234
00:10:03,518 --> 00:10:05,644
in 1966, and a beam of X-rays.

235
00:10:05,804 --> 00:10:08,205
And if a bar ever melts in your

236
00:10:08,205 --> 00:10:10,606
pocket, that was not a failure of chemistry.

237
00:10:10,606 --> 00:10:13,008
It was a very warm pocket. Put it

238
00:10:13,008 --> 00:10:15,409
in the fridge, wait, and accept that what

239
00:10:15,409 --> 00:10:17,810
comes back is a looser crystal with ambitions.

240
00:10:18,367 --> 00:10:19,866
Why does bread rise?

241
00:10:20,416 --> 00:10:23,404
Every hole in a slice of bread

242
00:10:23,404 --> 00:10:26,393
was first inflated by something alive. That

243
00:10:26,393 --> 00:10:29,381
something is yeast, a living single-celled fungus.

244
00:10:29,541 --> 00:10:32,257
In wet dough it feeds on sugars and

245
00:10:32,257 --> 00:10:34,973
gives off carbon dioxide gas, and the stretchy

246
00:10:34,973 --> 00:10:37,690
gluten network traps that gas as bubbles. The

247
00:10:37,690 --> 00:10:40,406
dough rises. In the oven the bubbles swell

248
00:10:40,406 --> 00:10:42,443
and the crumb sets around them.

249
00:10:42,603 --> 00:10:45,458
In 1857, Louis Pasteur argued that fermentation

250
00:10:45,458 --> 00:10:48,313
is the work of living cells. Today,

251
00:10:48,313 --> 00:10:51,169
X-ray scanners can watch the bubbles grow

252
00:10:51,169 --> 00:10:53,616
inside a loaf as it bakes.

253
00:10:54,166 --> 00:10:56,671
Mix flour and water, knead it, and leave

254
00:10:56,671 --> 00:10:59,176
it on the counter. Without yeast or another

255
00:10:59,176 --> 00:11:01,681
leavening, it will sit there. It will not

256
00:11:01,681 --> 00:11:04,186
grow. Bake it, and you get something closer

257
00:11:04,186 --> 00:11:06,065
to a cracker than a loaf.

258
00:11:06,225 --> 00:11:08,089
So the rise is not in

259
00:11:08,089 --> 00:11:09,643
the flour. It is added.

260
00:11:10,193 --> 00:11:12,481
For most of history, bakers added it

261
00:11:12,481 --> 00:11:14,769
without knowing what it was. A piece

262
00:11:14,769 --> 00:11:17,056
of yesterday's dough went into today's. A

263
00:11:17,056 --> 00:11:19,344
spoonful of foam from a brewer's vat

264
00:11:19,344 --> 00:11:21,305
went into the bowl. It worked.

265
00:11:21,465 --> 00:11:24,257
The dough swelled, soured slightly, and baked light.

266
00:11:24,257 --> 00:11:27,048
Bakers passed the habit down, along with the

267
00:11:27,048 --> 00:11:29,840
starter itself, the way a family passes down

268
00:11:29,840 --> 00:11:31,934
a recipe it cannot fully explain.

269
00:11:32,094 --> 00:11:34,441
Why it worked was a different question, and

270
00:11:34,441 --> 00:11:36,494
it took a long time to answer.

271
00:11:37,044 --> 00:11:40,101
In 1857, Louis Pasteur, then a young professor

272
00:11:40,101 --> 00:11:43,159
in Lille, presented a memoir on fermentation. Many

273
00:11:43,159 --> 00:11:46,216
chemists of the time thought fermentation was a

274
00:11:46,216 --> 00:11:49,274
purely chemical breakdown, a kind of slow decay

275
00:11:49,274 --> 00:11:51,567
that needed no life at all.

276
00:11:51,727 --> 00:11:54,851
Pasteur argued the opposite. Fermentation, he said, was

277
00:11:54,851 --> 00:11:57,975
the work of living microorganisms. By 1860 he

278
00:11:57,975 --> 00:12:01,099
had extended the argument to the fermentation that

279
00:12:01,099 --> 00:12:03,052
makes alcohol, and to yeast.

280
00:12:03,602 --> 00:12:06,276
His evidence was partly what he could see.

281
00:12:06,276 --> 00:12:08,951
Under the microscope, a fermenting liquid was full

282
00:12:08,951 --> 00:12:11,625
of small round cells, and those cells grew

283
00:12:11,625 --> 00:12:13,965
and multiplied as the fermentation went on.

284
00:12:14,125 --> 00:12:16,594
Where he kept living cells out, the

285
00:12:16,594 --> 00:12:19,063
fermentation did not start. Where he let

286
00:12:19,063 --> 00:12:21,533
them in, it did. The yeast was

287
00:12:21,533 --> 00:12:24,002
not a by-product of the process. It

288
00:12:24,002 --> 00:12:26,471
was the process. That sounds obvious now.

289
00:12:26,631 --> 00:12:28,495
At the time it was a fight, and

290
00:12:28,495 --> 00:12:30,360
the people on the other side were some

291
00:12:30,360 --> 00:12:31,991
of the most respected chemists in Europe.

292
00:12:32,541 --> 00:12:35,126
Watch the dough on the screen. Pasteur

293
00:12:35,126 --> 00:12:37,711
never saw a loaf rise in a

294
00:12:37,711 --> 00:12:40,297
scanner. He saw cells under glass, multiplying,

295
00:12:40,297 --> 00:12:42,882
and he trusted what they told him.

296
00:12:43,432 --> 00:12:45,689
The yeast in most bread is a

297
00:12:45,689 --> 00:12:47,945
species called Saccharomyces cerevisiae. Each cell is

298
00:12:47,945 --> 00:12:50,202
a tiny oval, far too small to

299
00:12:50,202 --> 00:12:51,491
see without a microscope.

300
00:12:51,651 --> 00:12:54,314
In dough, it feeds on sugars, some

301
00:12:54,314 --> 00:12:56,978
from the flour itself, some released from

302
00:12:56,978 --> 00:12:59,261
starch by the flour's own enzymes.

303
00:12:59,811 --> 00:13:02,767
Without much oxygen, yeast breaks those sugars

304
00:13:02,767 --> 00:13:05,723
down by fermentation. Two main things come

305
00:13:05,723 --> 00:13:08,680
out. Ethanol, the same alcohol as in

306
00:13:08,680 --> 00:13:11,636
beer, and carbon dioxide, a gas. In

307
00:13:11,636 --> 00:13:14,592
a brewery the alcohol is the point.

308
00:13:14,752 --> 00:13:16,792
In a bakery the gas is the

309
00:13:16,792 --> 00:13:18,833
point. The alcohol mostly leaves the loaf

310
00:13:18,833 --> 00:13:20,582
in the oven, along with steam.

311
00:13:21,132 --> 00:13:23,662
Look at the dough on the screen. Nothing

312
00:13:23,662 --> 00:13:26,192
will ever be pumped into it from outside.

313
00:13:26,192 --> 00:13:28,722
Countless cells inside it will breathe out the

314
00:13:28,722 --> 00:13:30,620
gas, very slowly, all at once.

315
00:13:31,170 --> 00:13:34,371
Gas on its own would simply escape. Something

316
00:13:34,371 --> 00:13:37,572
has to hold it. In wheat dough that

317
00:13:37,572 --> 00:13:40,773
something is gluten, the protein network wheat chemists,

318
00:13:40,773 --> 00:13:43,974
including those at the USDA Agricultural Research Service,

319
00:13:43,974 --> 00:13:45,575
have studied for generations.

320
00:13:45,735 --> 00:13:48,294
When wheat flour is wetted and worked, two

321
00:13:48,294 --> 00:13:50,852
families of its proteins link into a stretchy,

322
00:13:50,852 --> 00:13:53,411
elastic network, a little like a sheet of

323
00:13:53,411 --> 00:13:55,970
very thin balloon rubber running through the dough.

324
00:13:56,520 --> 00:13:59,033
Kneading lines that network up and strengthens it.

325
00:13:59,033 --> 00:14:01,546
The dough also carries tiny pockets of air,

326
00:14:01,546 --> 00:14:04,060
folded in while it was mixed. Those pockets

327
00:14:04,060 --> 00:14:06,573
are the seeds of every hole to come.

328
00:14:06,733 --> 00:14:08,848
The carbon dioxide from the yeast diffuses

329
00:14:08,848 --> 00:14:10,963
into them, and the gluten walls stretch

330
00:14:10,963 --> 00:14:13,079
to hold it. The dough grows. A

331
00:14:13,079 --> 00:14:15,194
dough with weak gluten lets the gas

332
00:14:15,194 --> 00:14:17,309
slip away and stays low and dense.

333
00:14:17,859 --> 00:14:19,997
Look at the dough on the screen

334
00:14:19,997 --> 00:14:22,135
as it stretches. That give is the

335
00:14:22,135 --> 00:14:24,273
gluten network, pulling thin around each pocket

336
00:14:24,273 --> 00:14:25,800
of gas instead of tearing.

337
00:14:26,350 --> 00:14:28,751
Yeast is alive, so it keeps its own

338
00:14:28,751 --> 00:14:31,152
time. In a warm kitchen it feeds quickly

339
00:14:31,152 --> 00:14:33,554
and the dough rises in an hour or

340
00:14:33,554 --> 00:14:35,955
two. In a cool room it slows down.

341
00:14:36,115 --> 00:14:38,255
In a refrigerator it slows almost to

342
00:14:38,255 --> 00:14:40,395
a stop, but it does not die.

343
00:14:40,395 --> 00:14:41,923
Bakers use that on purpose.

344
00:14:42,083 --> 00:14:44,590
A dough left overnight in the cold

345
00:14:44,590 --> 00:14:47,097
rises slowly, and the long, slow fermentation

346
00:14:47,097 --> 00:14:49,604
gives the yeast and the flour's own

347
00:14:49,604 --> 00:14:51,753
enzymes time to make more flavour.

348
00:14:52,303 --> 00:14:54,945
Too much warmth works against you. The yeast

349
00:14:54,945 --> 00:14:57,587
races, the gas comes too fast for the

350
00:14:57,587 --> 00:15:00,229
gluten to hold, and the dough can rise

351
00:15:00,229 --> 00:15:02,871
and then sag. Salt slows the yeast a

352
00:15:02,871 --> 00:15:04,522
little and tightens the gluten.

353
00:15:04,682 --> 00:15:07,233
Sugar feeds it, up to a point.

354
00:15:07,233 --> 00:15:09,784
Every recipe is quietly a set of

355
00:15:09,784 --> 00:15:12,335
instructions for managing a small, living population.

356
00:15:12,885 --> 00:15:15,429
On the screen, the loaves have risen.

357
00:15:15,429 --> 00:15:17,973
Nothing was pumped into them. Every hole

358
00:15:17,973 --> 00:15:20,154
inside them was made from within.

359
00:15:20,704 --> 00:15:23,467
In the oven, the rise speeds up for

360
00:15:23,467 --> 00:15:26,229
a few minutes. Bakers call it oven spring.

361
00:15:26,229 --> 00:15:28,992
Heat makes the gas in every bubble expand.

362
00:15:28,992 --> 00:15:31,755
Carbon dioxide that was dissolved in the dough

363
00:15:31,755 --> 00:15:33,136
comes out of solution.

364
00:15:33,296 --> 00:15:35,745
Water and alcohol turn to vapour and

365
00:15:35,745 --> 00:15:38,193
push the bubbles wider still. Then the

366
00:15:38,193 --> 00:15:40,642
heat catches up with the yeast, and

367
00:15:40,642 --> 00:15:42,741
the yeast dies. The rise stops.

368
00:15:43,291 --> 00:15:46,135
Now the structure sets. Starch granules in

369
00:15:46,135 --> 00:15:48,980
the dough soak up water and swell.

370
00:15:48,980 --> 00:15:51,824
The gluten proteins firm. The thin walls

371
00:15:51,824 --> 00:15:54,262
between the bubbles stiffen into place.

372
00:15:54,422 --> 00:15:56,294
By the time the centre of the

373
00:15:56,294 --> 00:15:58,166
loaf is near the boiling point of

374
00:15:58,166 --> 00:16:00,038
water, one hundred degrees Celsius, the crumb

375
00:16:00,038 --> 00:16:01,910
has set around its holes and will

376
00:16:01,910 --> 00:16:03,515
keep its shape as it cools.

377
00:16:04,065 --> 00:16:06,190
On the screen, the loaves sit in

378
00:16:06,190 --> 00:16:08,315
the heat. Inside every one of them

379
00:16:08,315 --> 00:16:10,440
the same quiet sequence runs, from rise,

380
00:16:10,440 --> 00:16:11,654
to spring, to set.

381
00:16:12,204 --> 00:16:14,757
For a long time, all of this was

382
00:16:14,757 --> 00:16:17,309
inferred from loaves cut open after the fact.

383
00:16:17,309 --> 00:16:19,862
You could see the holes, but not how

384
00:16:19,862 --> 00:16:22,414
they got there. X-ray tomography changed that. It

385
00:16:22,414 --> 00:16:24,967
is the same idea as a medical scanner.

386
00:16:25,127 --> 00:16:27,902
X-rays pass through the dough from

387
00:16:27,902 --> 00:16:30,677
many angles, and a computer rebuilds

388
00:16:30,677 --> 00:16:33,452
a three-dimensional picture of the inside.

389
00:16:34,002 --> 00:16:36,833
Researchers have put dough into these scanners and

390
00:16:36,833 --> 00:16:39,663
baked it there, imaging it again and again

391
00:16:39,663 --> 00:16:42,494
as it rose. The images show the bubbles

392
00:16:42,494 --> 00:16:45,324
growing. They show neighbouring bubbles touching and merging.

393
00:16:45,324 --> 00:16:47,447
And they show a turning point.

394
00:16:47,607 --> 00:16:50,315
Early on, each bubble is closed, like a

395
00:16:50,315 --> 00:16:53,023
foam. Later the walls between bubbles open up,

396
00:16:53,023 --> 00:16:55,731
and the crumb becomes a connected sponge, so

397
00:16:55,731 --> 00:16:58,439
steam can escape instead of bursting the loaf.

398
00:16:58,989 --> 00:17:01,339
That is why a slice of bread looks

399
00:17:01,339 --> 00:17:03,689
the way it does. Look closely at the

400
00:17:03,689 --> 00:17:06,040
slice on the screen. Every hole is a

401
00:17:06,040 --> 00:17:08,390
bubble that stopped growing at the moment the

402
00:17:08,390 --> 00:17:09,565
walls around it set.

403
00:17:09,725 --> 00:17:12,260
The large, ragged holes are bubbles that merged.

404
00:17:12,260 --> 00:17:14,795
The fine, even ones are bubbles that were

405
00:17:14,795 --> 00:17:16,696
held apart until the crumb set.

406
00:17:17,246 --> 00:17:19,355
Look at the crumb on the screen.

407
00:17:19,355 --> 00:17:21,465
Each hole is a frozen moment, the

408
00:17:21,465 --> 00:17:23,574
exact size its bubble had reached when

409
00:17:23,574 --> 00:17:25,081
the walls around it set.

410
00:17:25,631 --> 00:17:28,121
One part of the loaf never reaches

411
00:17:28,121 --> 00:17:30,611
the quiet ending. The inside stays moist

412
00:17:30,611 --> 00:17:33,101
and never gets much hotter than boiling

413
00:17:33,101 --> 00:17:35,591
water. The surface dries out and keeps

414
00:17:35,591 --> 00:17:37,370
heating, well past that point.

415
00:17:37,530 --> 00:17:40,264
There, the sugars and proteins of the dough

416
00:17:40,264 --> 00:17:42,998
start to react with each other, and the

417
00:17:42,998 --> 00:17:45,732
crust turns brown and starts to smell like

418
00:17:45,732 --> 00:17:48,466
bread. That reaction has a name, and it

419
00:17:48,466 --> 00:17:50,175
belongs to the next question.

420
00:17:50,725 --> 00:17:53,384
The glowing cross-section is a drawing made to

421
00:17:53,384 --> 00:17:56,043
show where the gas cells sit, not a

422
00:17:56,043 --> 00:17:58,703
scan, laid over a computer-made picture. The dough,

423
00:17:58,703 --> 00:18:01,362
the loaves and the crumb are real footage,

424
00:18:01,362 --> 00:18:04,021
repeated into walls and mirrors to show scale.

425
00:18:04,571 --> 00:18:07,493
This explains a yeasted wheat loaf. Sourdough adds

426
00:18:07,493 --> 00:18:10,415
bacteria that sour the dough as well as

427
00:18:10,415 --> 00:18:13,337
raise it. Rye and gluten-free breads hold their

428
00:18:13,337 --> 00:18:15,894
gas in other ways, with different structures.

429
00:18:16,054 --> 00:18:18,244
And the X-ray work is done on

430
00:18:18,244 --> 00:18:20,434
small samples in special ovens. The exact

431
00:18:20,434 --> 00:18:22,623
moment a foam opens into a sponge

432
00:18:22,623 --> 00:18:24,813
depends on the dough, the heat and

433
00:18:24,813 --> 00:18:27,003
the recipe. The scanners show it happening.

434
00:18:27,163 --> 00:18:28,712
They do not give one

435
00:18:28,712 --> 00:18:29,952
number for every loaf.

436
00:18:30,502 --> 00:18:33,367
So a loaf is a record of a

437
00:18:33,367 --> 00:18:36,231
quiet event. Countless cells breathed out, a web

438
00:18:36,231 --> 00:18:39,096
of wheat protein held its breath, and an

439
00:18:39,096 --> 00:18:40,886
oven made the moment permanent.

440
00:18:41,046 --> 00:18:43,373
It is also, if you think about

441
00:18:43,373 --> 00:18:45,701
it, the only time most of us

442
00:18:45,701 --> 00:18:48,028
happily eat something because it was full

443
00:18:48,028 --> 00:18:49,691
of holes. Next, the crust.

444
00:18:49,851 --> 00:18:52,283
Something happens on the outside of a steak

445
00:18:52,283 --> 00:18:54,411
that the inside never gets to taste.

446
00:18:54,967 --> 00:18:57,223
Why does seared meat taste so good?

447
00:18:57,773 --> 00:19:00,406
The inside of a steak never gets hot

448
00:19:00,406 --> 00:19:03,038
enough to taste like its crust. The flavour

449
00:19:03,038 --> 00:19:05,671
lives on the surface. When the surface of

450
00:19:05,671 --> 00:19:08,304
the meat is hot and dry, its sugars

451
00:19:08,304 --> 00:19:09,949
and amino acids react together.

452
00:19:10,109 --> 00:19:13,639
They make brown pigments and a large family

453
00:19:13,639 --> 00:19:17,168
of savoury, roasted aroma compounds. That is the

454
00:19:17,168 --> 00:19:20,698
Maillard reaction. Louis-Camille Maillard reported it in 1912.

455
00:19:20,698 --> 00:19:24,228
John Hodge of the US Department of Agriculture

456
00:19:24,228 --> 00:19:26,434
mapped its steps in 1953.

457
00:19:26,594 --> 00:19:29,423
And gas chromatography can pull the smell of

458
00:19:29,423 --> 00:19:32,253
a crust apart, one molecule at a time.

459
00:19:32,803 --> 00:19:35,090
Take two pieces of the same beef.

460
00:19:35,090 --> 00:19:37,378
Boil one. Sear the other in a

461
00:19:37,378 --> 00:19:39,665
very hot pan. Both are cooked through.

462
00:19:39,665 --> 00:19:41,299
Both are safe to eat.

463
00:19:41,459 --> 00:19:43,781
But one is grey and mild, and the

464
00:19:43,781 --> 00:19:46,103
other is brown, crisp at the edges, and

465
00:19:46,103 --> 00:19:48,424
smells like dinner. Nothing was added to the

466
00:19:48,424 --> 00:19:50,456
seared piece. Something was made on it.

467
00:19:50,616 --> 00:19:52,598
And it was made in a layer so

468
00:19:52,598 --> 00:19:54,581
thin you could scrape it off with a

469
00:19:54,581 --> 00:19:56,563
fingernail, if you could bear to waste it.

470
00:19:57,113 --> 00:19:59,732
The difference is water. Water boils at one

471
00:19:59,732 --> 00:20:02,350
hundred degrees Celsius at sea level. As long

472
00:20:02,350 --> 00:20:04,969
as the surface of the meat is wet,

473
00:20:04,969 --> 00:20:07,587
it cannot get much hotter than that, because

474
00:20:07,587 --> 00:20:10,206
the heat goes into turning water into steam.

475
00:20:10,366 --> 00:20:12,555
A boiled piece of meat spends its

476
00:20:12,555 --> 00:20:14,744
whole life at or below that ceiling.

477
00:20:14,744 --> 00:20:16,933
A seared one dries its surface first,

478
00:20:16,933 --> 00:20:18,809
and then climbs well past it.

479
00:20:19,359 --> 00:20:21,537
That is why cooks pat meat dry

480
00:20:21,537 --> 00:20:23,715
before it goes in the pan, and

481
00:20:23,715 --> 00:20:25,893
why a crowded pan makes grey meat.

482
00:20:25,893 --> 00:20:28,070
Too many pieces release too much water,

483
00:20:28,070 --> 00:20:29,626
and the surface stays wet.

484
00:20:29,786 --> 00:20:31,502
The browning only begins once

485
00:20:31,502 --> 00:20:32,874
the water is gone.

486
00:20:33,424 --> 00:20:36,025
Listen to the pan on the screen. That

487
00:20:36,025 --> 00:20:38,626
hiss is water leaving the surface of the

488
00:20:38,626 --> 00:20:41,227
meat. Until it stops, the browning cannot begin.

489
00:20:41,777 --> 00:20:45,370
In 1912, a French chemist named Louis-Camille Maillard

490
00:20:45,370 --> 00:20:48,963
reported an experiment to the French Academy of

491
00:20:48,963 --> 00:20:52,557
Sciences. He heated solutions of sugars together with

492
00:20:52,557 --> 00:20:56,150
amino acids, the small building blocks of proteins.

493
00:20:56,310 --> 00:20:58,857
The mixtures turned yellow, then brown, and

494
00:20:58,857 --> 00:21:01,041
gave off gas and new smells.

495
00:21:01,591 --> 00:21:04,228
Maillard was not trying to improve a

496
00:21:04,228 --> 00:21:06,865
recipe. He was interested in chemistry inside

497
00:21:06,865 --> 00:21:09,502
living things, and in how proteins and

498
00:21:09,502 --> 00:21:11,762
sugars might combine in the body.

499
00:21:11,922 --> 00:21:13,708
The kitchen had been running his reaction

500
00:21:13,708 --> 00:21:15,495
for as long as anyone had held

501
00:21:15,495 --> 00:21:17,281
meat over a fire. He was the

502
00:21:17,281 --> 00:21:19,067
one who wrote it down and gave

503
00:21:19,067 --> 00:21:20,343
it a place in chemistry.

504
00:21:20,503 --> 00:21:22,741
What Maillard found was not a new flavour.

505
00:21:22,741 --> 00:21:24,978
It was a name for an old one.

506
00:21:25,528 --> 00:21:27,706
Look at the crust on the screen.

507
00:21:27,706 --> 00:21:29,883
Every brown speck on it is a

508
00:21:29,883 --> 00:21:32,061
small record of the reaction he described.

509
00:21:32,611 --> 00:21:35,238
For decades the reaction stayed a tangle.

510
00:21:35,238 --> 00:21:37,864
Many chemists studied browning, especially in dried

511
00:21:37,864 --> 00:21:40,491
and stored foods, which darkened and changed

512
00:21:40,491 --> 00:21:41,992
flavour on the shelf.

513
00:21:42,152 --> 00:21:44,746
In 1953, John Hodge, a chemist at

514
00:21:44,746 --> 00:21:47,340
a US Department of Agriculture laboratory in

515
00:21:47,340 --> 00:21:49,935
Peoria, Illinois, published a single scheme that

516
00:21:49,935 --> 00:21:52,529
pulled the work together, in the Journal

517
00:21:52,529 --> 00:21:54,382
of Agricultural and Food Chemistry.

518
00:21:54,932 --> 00:21:58,115
Hodge divided the reaction into stages. First, a

519
00:21:58,115 --> 00:22:01,299
sugar joins an amino acid. That first product

520
00:22:01,299 --> 00:22:04,482
rearranges into a more stable form. Then it

521
00:22:04,482 --> 00:22:07,268
breaks apart into smaller, very reactive pieces.

522
00:22:07,428 --> 00:22:09,895
Those pieces react with each other and

523
00:22:09,895 --> 00:22:12,363
with more amino acids, making the brown

524
00:22:12,363 --> 00:22:14,830
pigments and the smells. His diagram is

525
00:22:14,830 --> 00:22:17,298
still drawn in food chemistry teaching today,

526
00:22:17,298 --> 00:22:19,060
with his name on it.

527
00:22:19,610 --> 00:22:22,526
One step in that web matters a great

528
00:22:22,526 --> 00:22:25,442
deal for meat. Some of the reactive pieces

529
00:22:25,442 --> 00:22:28,358
tear amino acids apart in a step called

530
00:22:28,358 --> 00:22:31,274
Strecker degradation. That releases small, volatile molecules that

531
00:22:31,274 --> 00:22:33,461
smell strongly, even at tiny amounts.

532
00:22:33,621 --> 00:22:36,706
Amino acids that carry sulfur add sulfur

533
00:22:36,706 --> 00:22:39,791
compounds, which give the deep, meaty notes.

534
00:22:40,341 --> 00:22:42,884
On the screen, the meat keeps searing.

535
00:22:42,884 --> 00:22:45,426
Every step of Hodge's map is running

536
00:22:45,426 --> 00:22:47,969
at once on that surface, all tangled

537
00:22:47,969 --> 00:22:50,512
together, faster than any diagram could show.

538
00:22:51,062 --> 00:22:53,896
A crust smells like one thing. It is

539
00:22:53,896 --> 00:22:56,730
actually a crowd. To see the crowd, chemists

540
00:22:56,730 --> 00:22:59,563
use gas chromatography. The aroma is collected from

541
00:22:59,563 --> 00:23:02,397
the cooked meat and sent through a very

542
00:23:02,397 --> 00:23:03,814
long, very thin column.

543
00:23:03,974 --> 00:23:06,850
Different molecules travel through it at different speeds.

544
00:23:06,850 --> 00:23:09,726
They come out the far end one by

545
00:23:09,726 --> 00:23:11,883
one, each at its own moment.

546
00:23:12,433 --> 00:23:15,235
Picture the steak cut open, with a glowing

547
00:23:15,235 --> 00:23:18,037
line along the thin brown band at the

548
00:23:18,037 --> 00:23:20,839
edge. That band is where nearly all of

549
00:23:20,839 --> 00:23:23,640
this chemistry happens. The pale interior never got

550
00:23:23,640 --> 00:23:26,092
hot or dry enough to join in.

551
00:23:26,642 --> 00:23:29,317
Joined to a mass spectrometer, the instrument weighs

552
00:23:29,317 --> 00:23:31,992
each molecule as it comes out and identifies

553
00:23:31,992 --> 00:23:34,667
it. In some laboratories, a person sits at

554
00:23:34,667 --> 00:23:37,342
a second outlet and sniffs, calling out what

555
00:23:37,342 --> 00:23:38,679
each one smells like.

556
00:23:38,839 --> 00:23:41,664
Studies of roast beef aroma have used this

557
00:23:41,664 --> 00:23:44,489
kind of separation to identify sulfur compounds present

558
00:23:44,489 --> 00:23:47,314
in tiny amounts that still shape the smell.

559
00:23:47,864 --> 00:23:51,485
What comes out is a family of compounds.

560
00:23:51,485 --> 00:23:55,106
Pyrazines, with roasted, nutty notes. Sulfur compounds, savoury

561
00:23:55,106 --> 00:23:58,727
and meaty. Furans, sweet and caramel-like. No single

562
00:23:58,727 --> 00:24:01,896
molecule is the smell of a steak.

563
00:24:02,056 --> 00:24:04,666
It is the mixture, in its proportions,

564
00:24:04,666 --> 00:24:07,276
that your nose recognises. Change the heat

565
00:24:07,276 --> 00:24:09,887
or the time, and the proportions shift,

566
00:24:09,887 --> 00:24:12,497
which is why the same steak can

567
00:24:12,497 --> 00:24:15,107
taste quite different from two different cooks.

568
00:24:15,657 --> 00:24:18,067
Watch the meat on the screen turn in

569
00:24:18,067 --> 00:24:20,477
the heat. Each piece is building its own

570
00:24:20,477 --> 00:24:22,887
crowd of molecules, in its own proportions, in

571
00:24:22,887 --> 00:24:24,996
the thin brown layer at its surface.

572
00:24:25,546 --> 00:24:27,843
If the Maillard reaction makes the savoury,

573
00:24:27,843 --> 00:24:30,140
roasted part of the smell, why does

574
00:24:30,140 --> 00:24:32,436
seared lamb not smell like seared beef?

575
00:24:32,436 --> 00:24:34,405
Part of the answer is fat.

576
00:24:34,565 --> 00:24:37,197
As meat heats, its fats break down

577
00:24:37,197 --> 00:24:39,830
too, especially where they meet oxygen, and

578
00:24:39,830 --> 00:24:42,462
they make their own family of aroma

579
00:24:42,462 --> 00:24:45,095
molecules. Those fragments also join the Maillard

580
00:24:45,095 --> 00:24:46,975
reactions, and the mixture changes.

581
00:24:47,525 --> 00:24:50,259
Fats differ from one animal to the next,

582
00:24:50,259 --> 00:24:52,993
so the fat-derived notes differ too. The Maillard

583
00:24:52,993 --> 00:24:55,727
reaction gives the meal its roasted, meaty base.

584
00:24:55,727 --> 00:24:58,461
The fat writes the accent that tells you

585
00:24:58,461 --> 00:25:00,170
which animal it came from.

586
00:25:00,330 --> 00:25:02,746
Reviews of cooked meat aroma group the

587
00:25:02,746 --> 00:25:05,162
compounds this way: those from the browning

588
00:25:05,162 --> 00:25:07,577
reaction, those from the fat, and those

589
00:25:07,577 --> 00:25:09,648
that come from both at once.

590
00:25:10,198 --> 00:25:12,251
Watch the fat on the screen as

591
00:25:12,251 --> 00:25:14,305
it sizzles and spits. It is not

592
00:25:14,305 --> 00:25:16,358
only cooking the meat. It is adding

593
00:25:16,358 --> 00:25:18,118
its own molecules to the smell.

594
00:25:18,668 --> 00:25:21,541
Once you know what to look for, the

595
00:25:21,541 --> 00:25:24,413
reaction is everywhere in a kitchen. The brown

596
00:25:24,413 --> 00:25:27,286
crust of the loaf in the last question

597
00:25:27,286 --> 00:25:29,799
is the Maillard reaction. So is toast.

598
00:25:29,959 --> 00:25:32,772
So is the colour and much of the

599
00:25:32,772 --> 00:25:35,585
smell of roasted coffee beans and roasted nuts.

600
00:25:35,585 --> 00:25:38,397
In each case, sugars and amino acids meet

601
00:25:38,397 --> 00:25:41,210
on a hot, dry surface, and the same

602
00:25:41,210 --> 00:25:44,023
family of brown pigments and aroma molecules appears.

603
00:25:44,573 --> 00:25:47,042
That is part of why the smell of

604
00:25:47,042 --> 00:25:49,510
something browning is so easy to recognise, and

605
00:25:49,510 --> 00:25:51,979
so hard to describe. You are smelling the

606
00:25:51,979 --> 00:25:54,448
same chemistry in different proportions, tuned by the

607
00:25:54,448 --> 00:25:55,682
food it happens on.

608
00:25:55,842 --> 00:25:58,496
A cook who sears, toasts or roasts

609
00:25:58,496 --> 00:26:01,149
is steering one reaction toward different ends.

610
00:26:01,699 --> 00:26:04,676
On the screen, the grills go on and

611
00:26:04,676 --> 00:26:07,653
on, and the sound is the same everywhere:

612
00:26:07,653 --> 00:26:09,886
water leaving, and the browning beginning.

613
00:26:10,436 --> 00:26:13,352
Two other things also turn food brown, and

614
00:26:13,352 --> 00:26:16,269
they are easy to confuse with this one.

615
00:26:16,269 --> 00:26:19,185
Caramelisation is sugar on its own breaking down

616
00:26:19,185 --> 00:26:21,737
under heat, with no amino acids involved.

617
00:26:21,897 --> 00:26:24,656
Charring is what happens past both, when

618
00:26:24,656 --> 00:26:27,414
the surface simply burns. A good crust

619
00:26:27,414 --> 00:26:30,173
lives between them. It needs heat and

620
00:26:30,173 --> 00:26:32,537
dryness. It does not need fire.

621
00:26:33,087 --> 00:26:35,507
Watch the sear on the screen. The

622
00:26:35,507 --> 00:26:37,927
pan is quiet now. The browning has

623
00:26:37,927 --> 00:26:40,347
moved slowly across the surface, and the

624
00:26:40,347 --> 00:26:42,767
smell is the reaction itself, drifting up.

625
00:26:43,317 --> 00:26:45,745
The glowing cross-section is a drawing showing

626
00:26:45,745 --> 00:26:48,173
where the crust sits, laid over a

627
00:26:48,173 --> 00:26:50,600
computer-made picture. The steaks, the pans and

628
00:26:50,600 --> 00:26:53,028
the flames are real footage, repeated into

629
00:26:53,028 --> 00:26:55,109
walls and mirrors to show scale.

630
00:26:55,659 --> 00:26:57,832
The chemistry of the reaction is well

631
00:26:57,832 --> 00:27:00,005
mapped. The full list of what forms

632
00:27:00,005 --> 00:27:02,179
in a given crust, and in what

633
00:27:02,179 --> 00:27:04,352
amounts, depends on the cut, the fat,

634
00:27:04,352 --> 00:27:05,904
the heat and the time.

635
00:27:06,064 --> 00:27:08,411
Identifying a molecule is not the same

636
00:27:08,411 --> 00:27:10,757
as knowing how much it matters to

637
00:27:10,757 --> 00:27:13,104
the flavour. Researchers still debate which few

638
00:27:13,104 --> 00:27:15,115
compounds carry most of the smell.

639
00:27:15,275 --> 00:27:17,667
And taste, in the end, is measured

640
00:27:17,667 --> 00:27:20,059
on people, who do not always agree.

641
00:27:20,609 --> 00:27:22,762
So the best part of a steak

642
00:27:22,762 --> 00:27:24,915
is a chemistry experiment that happens in

643
00:27:24,915 --> 00:27:27,067
the last millimetres of its surface, first

644
00:27:27,067 --> 00:27:29,220
written down by a man who was

645
00:27:29,220 --> 00:27:30,758
thinking about the human body,

646
00:27:30,918 --> 00:27:33,002
and mapped by a government chemist worried

647
00:27:33,002 --> 00:27:34,788
about dried food on a shelf.

648
00:27:34,948 --> 00:27:37,103
Neither of them, as far as we

649
00:27:37,103 --> 00:27:39,258
know, was trying to make dinner better.

650
00:27:39,258 --> 00:27:41,413
They did it anyway, and every cook

651
00:27:41,413 --> 00:27:42,644
since has been grateful.

652
00:27:43,200 --> 00:27:45,725
And that is all three: a square of

653
00:27:45,725 --> 00:27:48,249
chocolate, a loaf and a steak. A fat

654
00:27:48,249 --> 00:27:50,774
that picks one crystal out of six, a

655
00:27:50,774 --> 00:27:53,298
fungus that breathes, and a reaction that only

656
00:27:53,298 --> 00:27:55,507
happens where it is hot and dry.

657
00:27:55,667 --> 00:27:57,966
If you have made it this far, you

658
00:27:57,966 --> 00:28:00,266
now know more about your dinner than most

659
00:28:00,266 --> 00:28:02,565
of the people who cooked it for you,

660
00:28:02,725 --> 00:28:04,348
which is a lovely thing to

661
00:28:04,348 --> 00:28:05,972
know and a terrible thing to

662
00:28:05,972 --> 00:28:07,595
say out loud at the table.

663
00:28:07,755 --> 00:28:10,092
Keep it to yourself. Eat the crust

664
00:28:10,092 --> 00:28:12,430
first. Let the chocolate melt on your

665
00:28:12,430 --> 00:28:14,099
tongue, not in your hand.

666
00:28:14,259 --> 00:28:16,419
And next time, we will find out

667
00:28:16,419 --> 00:28:18,579
what happens inside an egg, which turns

668
00:28:18,579 --> 00:28:20,740
out to be stranger than anything in

669
00:28:20,740 --> 00:28:22,900
this video. Thank you for watching all

670
00:28:22,900 --> 00:28:24,443
the way to the end.

671
00:28:24,603 --> 00:28:26,657
If there is a food you have always

672
00:28:26,657 --> 00:28:28,710
wondered about, tell us in the comments, and

673
00:28:28,710 --> 00:28:30,507
it might become one of the questions.
